WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.365
Reconnaissance is the information-gathering stage that may happen before visible activity inside the organization.

00:00:05.365 --> 00:00:10.254
A defender may never see all of it, which is why preparation matters.

00:00:10.254 --> 00:00:13.639
The defensive question is not how to perform reconnaissance;

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:19.656
it is what useful information the organization is exposing and how that exposure can be reduced.

00:00:19.656 --> 00:00:23.793
Public-facing systems, stale DNS records, forgotten test services, exposed contact information,

00:00:23.793 --> 00:00:27.929
unmanaged cloud assets, and vague ownership all make later analysis harder.

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:31.314
Good defensive preparation starts with asset inventory, ownership records,

00:00:31.314 --> 00:00:33.946
external exposure review, patch and configuration hygiene,

00:00:33.946 --> 00:00:38.083
and monitoring for unusual probing against systems the organization cares about.

00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:42.596
Analysts should also understand what normal internet noise looks like so they

00:00:42.596 --> 00:00:46.357
can distinguish routine scanning from activity that deserves closer review.

00:00:46.357 --> 00:00:50.494
Reconnaissance is an early-stage concept, but the defensive work is ongoing.

00:00:50.494 --> 00:00:52.750
Keep inventories current, remove unnecessary exposure,

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:58.015
validate who owns internet-facing services, and make sure logs exist before an incident begins.

00:00:58.015 --> 00:00:59.519
When later evidence appears,

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:04.032
that preparation helps the team answer practical questions quickly: what was exposed,

00:01:04.032 --> 00:01:08.169
what changed, what was targeted, and which controls were in place.

00:01:08.169 --> 00:01:11.178
Preparation also makes later incident notes more reliable.

00:01:11.178 --> 00:01:15.690
If the team already knows which assets are exposed, who owns them,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.571
and which logs are available,

00:01:17.571 --> 00:01:22.084
analysts spend less time reconstructing basic context and more time understanding whether

00:01:22.084 --> 00:01:24.340
the current activity represents meaningful risk.
